    "body": "@annevk Taking this as an example https://codepen.io/patrickhlauke/pen/raWGgRm the problematic case I often encounter is where `legend` is not the immediate first child of the `fieldset` ... something like\n\n```\n<fieldset>\n  <div>\n    <legend>The legend</legend>\n  </div>\n  ...\n</fieldset>\n```\n\nIn Chrome, Firefox, and Safari when inspecting the accessibility tree / testing with a screen reader, the `fieldset` lacks an accessible name, i.e. the `legend` is not used to provide the accName (with all the related behaviours that follow from that when using screen readers - normally, when you jump into a group with an accessible name, screen readers will announce the accName for the group as you enter it; in this case, none of them do)\n\nI can imagine that the decision about \"it must be the first child\" came perhaps because of the old default styling for fieldset/legend, where the legend is \"hoisted\" visually into the fieldset border\n\n![screenshot of a standard fieldset/legend, where the fieldset has a border, and the legend is shoved visually to be on top of the top edge of the border](https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/a38b1c0f-d3cf-44cf-a264-d2f349ce10e3)\n\nThis visual effect would get trickier/possibly illogical if the `legend` was in any deeper structure. But I'll be honest, I'd even be ok if the default visual styling for a deeper legend explicitly did *not* do this visual effect of hoisting the legend onto the border, just as long as behind the scenes it still used the `legend` to provide the accessible name for its `fieldset`",
    "author_association": "MEMBER",
    "pin": null,
    "reactions": {
