    "body": "Just to add that, anecdotally, I come across the problem here often when auditing content for accessibility.\n\nEither authors are unaware that `legend` MUST be a direct child element of `fieldset` and wrap it in extra stuff (like the above examples, and then fail accessibility assessments because the group/fieldset lacks an accessible name), *or* they are aware of the problem and are forced to use `aria-labelledby` (and the related pain of generating/inventing an `id`) because they want/need a more complex markup structure (usually to allow them to achieve a particular styling/look) that won't allow them to have `legend` as the immediate child. Things like:\n\n```html\n<fieldset aria-labelledby=\"fieldset-legend-1\">\n  <div ...>\n    <div ..>\n      <h3 id=\"fieldset-legend-1\">Blah</h3>\n      ...\n    </div>\n    ...\n  </div>\n  ...\n</fieldset>\n```\n\nIt seems counterintuitive to force authors down that path for this relatively common case.\n\nIt shouldn't be beyond the capability of browsers (and hopefully not too expensive?) to have an algorithm along the lines of:\n\n* for the `fieldset`, start looking at all child elements\n* if you haven't found a `legend`, go into the child elements of the child elements, recursively\n* abort that particular dive into a particular subtree if you encounter another `fieldset` or `optgroup` (and this is where you could hook into any future exclusions)\n* if you find a `legend`, abort and use this as the accessible name for the `fieldset`\n* stop once you're traversed the entire subtree of the `fieldset` - if you haven't found a suitable `legend`, the `fieldset` lacks an accessible name",
